## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests a foundational dynamic programming pattern—selecting k non-adjacent elements with maximum sum. It assesses understanding of state definition, transition logic, and reconstruction, making it a classic beginner DP problem.

## Key Concepts
- **State definition**: dp[i][j] represents the optimal sum for the first i elements with exactly j selections
- **Transition logic**: Compare skipping the current element vs. taking it (with the appropriate previous state)
- **Backtracking**: Reconstruct chosen indices by comparing states during backtracking
- **Feasibility constraint**: Maximum selections possible in i elements is floor((i+1)/2)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumNonAdjacent(A, m) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(m + 1).fill(NEG));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j <= Math.min(m, Math.floor((i + 1) / 2)); j++) {
      const take = A[i - 1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2][j - 1] : (j - 1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG));
      const skip = dp[i - 1][j];
      dp[i][j] = Math.max(skip, take);
    }
  }

  const result = dp[n][m];
  const indices = [];
  let i = n, j = m;

  while (i > 0 && j > 0) {
    const take = A[i - 1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2][j - 1] : (j - 1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG));
    if (dp[i][j] === take) {
      indices.push(i - 1);
      j--;
      i -= 2;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { sum: result, indices: indices.reverse() };
}
```

**Time Complexity:** O(n × m)
**Space Complexity:** O(n × m), or O(m) with optimization.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend Kadane-like DP to k non-overlapping segments with per-element penalties and reconstruction of exact endpoints.

## Key Concepts
- DP for k disjoint subarrays with penalties
- Local/global state separation for endings vs global maxima
- Backpointer reconstruction to recover endpoints
- Time complexity O(nk), space O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, p) {
  const n = A.length;
  const negInf = -1e18;
  const local = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(negInf));
  const global = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(negInf));
  global[0][0] = 0;
  // backpointers omitted for brevity in this snippet
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const val = A[i - 1] - p;
      local[i][t] = Math.max(local[i - 1][t] + val, global[i - 1][t - 1] + val);
      global[i][t] = Math.max(global[i - 1][t], local[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return { maxValue: global[n][k], endpoints: [] };
}
```

