## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a DP over partitions where block value depends on both max and sum, plus reconstruction of segments. Encourages discussion on precomputation of range maxima and efficient transitions.

## Key Concepts

- DP over partitioning with fixed number of segments
- Block value using max and sum requires precomputation
- Path reconstruction for exact endpoints

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxBlockPartition(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  const pref=new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+A[i];
  // precompute max for all subarrays
  const maxSub=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ let m=A[i]; for(let j=i;j<n;j++){ m=Math.max(m,A[j]); maxSub[i][j]=m; } }
  const blockVal=(l,r)=> (r-l+1)*maxSub[l][r] - (pref[r+1]-pref[l]);
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t]=dp[i-1][t];
      par[i][t]=[i-1,t];
      for(let s=1;s<=i;s++){
        const val=dp[s-1][t-1] + blockVal(s-1,i-1);
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; par[i][t]=[s-1,t-1]; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would backtrack from (n,k) using par
  return {best: dp[n][k], par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to avoid O(n^2 k) in practice?  
- How does reconstruction behave with negative block values?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests concrete DP skills on string partitioning with reconstruction; approachable for beginners yet non-trivial.

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][t]: maximum sum for first i digits split into t parts
- val(j,i): integer value of s[j..i-1] (leading zeros allowed)
- transition: dp[i][t] = max_{j ∈ [t-1..i-1]} dp[j][t-1] + val(j,i)
- reconstruction: track prev to recover split indices
- complexity: O(n²k) time, O(nk) space; use BigInt for large results

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumSplit(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const val = (a,b)=> BigInt(s.slice(a,b));
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1n));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    dp[i][1] = val(0,i);
  }

  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let j=t-1;j<i;j++){
        const candidate = dp[j][t-1] + val(j,i);
        if(candidate > dp[i][t]){
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          prev[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct splits
  const splits = [];
  let curr = n, parts = k;
  while(parts > 1){
    const j = prev[curr][parts];
    splits.push(j);
    curr = j;
    parts--;
  }
  splits.reverse();

  return {maxSum: dp[n][k], splits};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests weighted interval scheduling with an exact k constraint and reconstruction; requires sorting, precomputing p(i), and a 2D DP with backtracking; also considerations for memory optimization.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling
- DP with an extra dimension for exact k selections
- Precomputing p(i) (latest non-overlapping job)
- Space-time tradeoffs; backtracking reconstruction
- Stability under large n and k; edge-case handling when K > possible non-overlapping sets

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKNonOverlapping(jobs, K) {
  // jobs: {s, e, r}; sort by end time
  jobs.sort((a,b)=> a.e - b.e);
  const n = jobs.length;
  const end = jobs.map(j=> j.e);
  // p(i): last index < i with end[p] < start[i]
  const p = new Array(n).fill(-1);
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) {
    let lo=0, hi=i-1, ans=-1;
    while (lo<=hi){
      const mid=(lo+hi)>>1;
      if (end[mid] < jobs[i].s){ ans=mid; lo=mid+1; } else hi=mid-1;
    }
    p[i]=ans;
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(false));
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for (let t=1; t<=K; t++){
      const without = dp[i-1][t];
      const withTake = (p[i-1] >= 0 ? dp[p[i-1]+1][t-1] : (t-1===0?0: -Infinity)) + jobs[i-1].r;
      if (withTake > without){ dp[i][t] = withTake; take[i][t] = true; } else { dp[i][t] = without; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  const res = [];
  let i=n, t=K;
  while (i>0 && t>0){
    if (take[i][t]){ res.push(i-1); i = (p[i-1] >= 0 ? p[i-1]+1 : 0); t--; }
    else { i--; }
  }
  res.reverse();
  return { revenue: dp[n][K], indices: res };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to an "at most K" constraint?
- How can you optimize memory to O(n + K) or O(K) and still reconstruct the solution?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests dynamic programming with multiple constraints: non-adjacent blocks, exact block count, and reconstruction requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Disjoint blocks with at least one-element gap between consecutive blocks
- dp[i][t] transitions for skipping elements vs. ending a block
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sum queries
- Backpointers for reconstructing the chosen subarray endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const prefix = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) prefix[i] = prefix[i - 1] + A[i - 1];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // Skip element i
      if (dp[i - 1][t] >= dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
        prev[i][t] = { skipped: true };
      }
      // End a block at i
      for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
        const prevIdx = s >= 2 ? s - 2 : 0;
        const val = dp[prevIdx][t - 1] + (prefix[i] - prefix[s - 1]) - c;
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = { skipped: false, start: s };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction
  const result = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    const state = prev[i][t];
    if (state.skipped) {
      i--;
    } else {
      result.push({ start: state.start, end: i });
      i = state.start - 2;
      t--;
    }
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks: result.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem extends classic knapsack-style dynamic programming to incorporate time-based constraints (deadlines) and a limit on the number of selectable jobs. It tests understanding of multi-dimensional DP states, proper handling of time as a resource, and the ability to reconstruct the solution.

## Key Concepts
- Scheduling with deadlines and profits
- 3D DP formulation tracking (jobs processed, jobs selected, time)
- Backward iteration to avoid reusing the same job
- Reconstruction via predecessor pointers
- Time complexity: O(n × K × D), where D is the maximum deadline

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfitJobs(jobs, K) {
  jobs.sort((a, b) => a.d - b.d);
  const maxT = Math.max(...jobs.map(j => j.d));
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => Array(maxT + 1).fill(NEG));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => Array(maxT + 1).fill(null));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (const { p, d, w } of jobs) {
    for (let k = K - 1; k >= 0; k--) {
      for (let t = d; t >= p; t--) {
        if (dp[k][t - p] !== NEG) {
          const newProfit = dp[k][t - p] + w;
          if (newProfit > dp[k + 1][t]) {
            dp[k + 1][t] = newProfit;
            prev[k + 1][t] = { prevK: k, prevT: t - p };
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  let bestProfit = 0, bestK = 0, bestT = 0;
  for (let k = 1; k <= K; k++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= maxT; t++) {
      if (dp[k][t] > bestProfit) {
        bestProfit = dp[k][t];
        bestK = k;
        bestT = t;
      }
    }
  }

  const selected = [];
  let [k, t] = [bestK, bestT];
  while (k > 0) {
    const { prevK, prevT } = prev[k][t];
    selected.push({ finishTime: t });
    k = prevK;
    t = prevT;
  }

  return { profit: bestProfit, jobs: selected.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the ability to model selections of multiple disjoint subarrays with an exact count — a common dynamic programming pattern in interviews.

## Key Concepts
- **DP on prefix**: State dp[i][t] tracks the best total using the first i elements with exactly t subarrays
- **Prefix sums**: Enable O(1) calculation of any subarray sum
- **Backpointers**: Store decisions to reconstruct the chosen subarrays
- **Edge cases**: Handle when n < k or when all values are negative

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + A[i - 1];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      if (dp[i - 1][t] >= dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
        prev[i][t] = { skip: true };
      }
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        if (t > 0 && dp[l - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity) {
          const val = dp[l - 1][t - 1] + (pref[i] - pref[l - 1]);
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            prev[i][t] = { skip: false, l, t: t - 1 };
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    const p = prev[i][t];
    if (p.skip) { i--; continue; }
    result.push([p.l, i]);
    i = p.l - 1; t = p.t;
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], subarrays: result.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a partition DP where segment values depend on a non-trivial per-substring metric (distinct counts). It extends simple sum-based DP to a more nuanced local score.

## Key Concepts
- DP over i and t to build up k segments
- Precompute distinctCount(l,r) for all substrings
- Backpointers to recover exact endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxDistinctSegments(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const cnt = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let L = 0; L < n; L++) {
    const freq = new Array(26).fill(0);
    let d = 0;
    for (let R = L; R < n; R++) {
      const idx = s.charCodeAt(R) - 97;
      if (freq[idx]++ === 0) d++;
      cnt[L][R] = d;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      for (let p = t-1; p <= i-1; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + cnt[p][i-1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct endpoints from par
}
```

